Used in: tools/k-labor/lib/prose/generic-pack.json
  All 115 entries of skills/humanize-korean/references/lexicon.json (v1.3: 61 "change",
  54 "conditional"). Each entry becomes one rule. The narrow "regex" and "regex_exclude"
  fields are copied unchanged, except:
  - L-05, L-54: the narrow regex drops "한" (L-05) and "작용" (L-54), as the upstream
    v2.4.3 refinement did for regex_detect.
  - L-08, L-54, INJ-03, INJ-09, INJ-29, INJ-30: the exclude list is the union of
    "regex_exclude" and "regex_detect_exclude" (upstream negative examples in
    test_detect_neg were otherwise matched by the narrow regex).
  - L-04, L-17, L-24, L-51: a Hangul (L-24: Hangul or Latin) boundary lookbehind is
    prepended to the alternative that starts with an unbounded character class, so
    that long runs without spaces stay linear time. That prefix alone would miss a
    match that starts right where the previous match ended inside such a run, so the
    entry also carries the unchanged upstream regex as "resume", and the runner
    retries it at exactly that position. The combined result equals the upstream regex.
  - AST-44, L-32, L-78, L-79, L-87, INJ-08: the trailing boundary ("\s" or "[,\s]")
    that the regex consumed is a lookahead instead. Matches start at the same positions
    as the upstream regex; only the boundary character is left out of the match.
  Headwords appear in the "fix" text. "skip_genres" is mapped to k-labor genres
  (일반 → general, 보고서 → consulting; 논문 has no counterpart). The exclude window
  (12 code points around each candidate) follows scripts/lexical_layer.py, but within
  one line only. Grades (all B), the legal-genre keep/change decisions, reasons and
  output policy are ours.

Used in: tests/fixtures/prose/imnotai-lexicon-examples.json
  The synthetic example sentences of the same 115 entries (test_positive,
